# Deep Modules & Clean Architecture

Expert software engineer and architect. All code generation, refactoring, and feature
implementations must adhere to John Ousterhout's principles from
*A Philosophy of Software Design*.

## 1. Deep modules over shallow modules

- **Maximize information hiding:** A module's interface must be simple and minimal; the
  internal implementation owns the complexity.
- **Forbid shallow wrappers:** Do not create thin wrapper classes, pass-through helpers, or
  single-use abstraction layers that raise cognitive overhead without encapsulation value.
- **High complexity-to-interface ratio:** If a function or class needs more docs/setup to
  understand than its logic performs, it is too shallow — collapse or redesign it.

## 2. Strict architectural boundaries

- **Unidirectional data flow:** UI / presentation → application/state layer → domain /
  business logic → data access / external systems.
- **Zero circular dependencies:** Modules must not depend on each other cyclically. Domain
  logic stays independent of framework-specific wiring (routing, UI hooks, DB drivers).

## 3. Code quality & refactoring constraints

- **Self-contained files:** One responsibility per module, with a clear predictable boundary.
- **Type safety:** Strict, explicit types (or language equivalents) on module inputs, outputs,
  and interfaces. No loose untyped boundaries at architectural seams.
- **Illegal states:** Make them unrepresentable (discriminated unions, required fields,
  parse at the edge). Design signatures so the failure cannot occur; fail explicitly
  when it still can.
- **Error translation:** Translate at the owning layer. Domain and UI never see
  driver errors, vendor payloads, or stack traces. Do not swallow; log at the owner;
  callers get a typed empty/error fallback.
- **Interface tests:** New behavior must be unit-testable at the module interface
  without mocking the whole app.
- **Incremental refactoring:** When changing complex code, do not rewrite the whole file
  unless necessary. Isolate tangled logic into deep, testable components.
